# eHacking in-network DNS resolver (CoreDNS).
#
# Why this exists
# ---------------
# Containers have to reach the platform through Traefik, and Traefik can
# only be addressed by the *hostname* its routers match on — SNI and the
# Host header both carry it. Compose can make a container answer to extra
# names (the `aliases:` on traefik below), but only to a FINITE list. The
# catcher hands every student their own salt subdomain
# `<salt>.${CATCHER_HOST}`, which is an unbounded set, so that list can
# never be complete.
#
# Measured on this host before the resolver existed:
#
#     container -> getent hosts my.e-attacker.de   132.195.101.17  (correct)
#     container -> 132.195.101.17:443              REFUSED
#     container -> 132.195.101.17:10443            OPEN
#
# The name resolves to this machine's own public address, but rootless
# podman's egress traverses neither the host's nat/PREROUTING nor its
# nat/OUTPUT, so the firewall's 443->10443 redirect never applies and
# nothing answers on 443. No host firewall rule can fix that; the name has
# to resolve inside the compose network instead.
#
# The symptom was the OIDC SP's server-side discovery fetch of
# `https://<salt>.${CATCHER_HOST}/.well-known/openid-configuration`
# (AttackManager.startWebfinger) failing with `ConnectException:
# Connection refused`, which takes the whole mIdP family (ids-1..ids-4)
# with it. The same path carries out-of-band XXE and SSRF exfiltration
# from xml-sec, soap-sec, json-sec and rest-api-sec.
#
# What it does
# ------------
# Two rewrites, one per zone this platform serves. Both cover the apex and
# every subdomain, so `e-hacking.de`, `sp.e-hacking.de`,
# `x.challenge.recruiting.e-hacking.de`, `e-attacker.de` and any
# `<salt>.e-attacker.de` all land on Traefik.
#
# ---> These two lines are the only place the zones are written down
#      besides .env. Adding a hostname under either zone needs no change
#      here; adding a whole new zone does.
#
# The rewrite target is the *service name* `traefik`, not an address. The
# container runtime's own DNS (podman's aardvark-dns, Docker's embedded
# resolver) resolves it on every query, so this file needs no static IP
# and survives `compose up --force-recreate`.
#
# `answer auto` rewrites the owner name in the response back to the name
# the client asked for. Without it a client asking for
# `abc.e-attacker.de.` gets an answer owned by `traefik.`, which a strict
# resolver may drop as off-question.
#
# Everything else — service names like `victim-bot` or `couchdb`, and real
# internet names — is forwarded to the runtime's own resolver via this
# container's /etc/resolv.conf. That is what keeps service-name resolution
# working for every container pointed at us, and it is also how the
# rewritten `traefik` query gets answered.
#
# Note the trailing `\.$`: CoreDNS matches the fully qualified query name,
# which always ends in a dot. A pattern without it silently never matches
# and every query falls through to `forward` — which looks exactly like
# the resolver not being wired up at all.
#
# Debugging: add `log` to the block below and `just restart dns` to get one
# line per query. The `reload` plugin picks up edits within ~30s without a
# restart.

.:53 {
    rewrite name regex ^(.*\.)?e-hacking\.de\.$  traefik answer auto
    rewrite name regex ^(.*\.)?e-attacker\.de\.$ traefik answer auto

    forward . /etc/resolv.conf

    # Cap the TTL clients cache. The rewrite plugin hands out 600s by
    # default; if traefik is recreated onto a new address that would pin
    # clients to the dead one for ten minutes.
    cache 30

    reload
    errors
}
